t more effective but  travel bans do not work have never  worked are not going to work with this  variant or any other that will come up  in the future dr gurdasani what is your  opinion on this are the travel brands  premature an overreaction or or what's  your take on it  so by the time a paint is identified it  is widespread so you know when it was  identified many of us said yes it's  definitely here in england because of  the transport links between different  regions that doesn't mean that slowing  down import  doesn't help i mean certainly that helps  but not through travel bans through uh  screening at the border and strong  quarantined measures and not targeted at  single countries but rather  comprehensive ones that require  isolation and testing of all travelers  and those measures have been shown to be  highly effective in in very large  studies across many countries but also  with the caveat that they are effective  because you're going to slow down spread  but then are you going to do something  with that extra time that you gain  because ultimately cases will probably  come through unless you have sort of  very blanket restrictions like some  countries in the southeast have which  have kept variants out  and i don't see countries like the us  and uk doing very much else with that  time and that time needs to be urgently  used to  put in domestic measures that reduce  transmission to reduce existing case  ways to build healthcare capacity to  increase vaccination and boosting unless  that time is used to do that it's it's  sort of going to be wasted  so i'll ask you the question that we put  at the top of the show and  is this going to be the new normal dr  gurdasani should people continue to  expect travel bans uh i know that um  ahmadougwel oma doesn't necessarily  agree with them but should people be  expecting this to be their new normal  they should face travel bans every  couple of months because of the  coronavirus  i think it depends on the political will  i mean if there is a globally  coordinated effort towards elimination  then things like quarantines and travel  restrictions and other restrictions will  be in the shorter term  but and by shorter term i still mean  unfortunately  over the course of a few years you know  like we did with measles we eliminated  it but gradually  but if that's not the course that we're  choosing and if the course is living  with the virus then i'm afraid we're  dealing with longer-term restrictions so  paradoxically i find the focus on  short-term freedoms and short-term  economic grain gains has trumped the  science which means that we are living  in longer-term restrictions and much  much greater economic damage and i think  at this point it's time for politicians  and scientists to reflect on you know  can we make a globally coordinated  effort which requires short-term pain  but you know does not require us living  with a virus that's constantly mutating  to potentially become more transmissible  and more able to escape the tools that  we have so michael jacobs how do then  governments balance keeping their  economies afloat on one end  while protecting populations from  transmission and is there a strategy  this time around going to have to be  different than last time around  the strategies in countries that are  more vaccinated um  is different um so lockdowns are  are occurring less than they used to  although we've now seen new lockdowns in  certain european countries because  transmission has extended far enough but  that will be different in many countries  from before i think many industries have  become better at adapting to the  situation where people can work from  home people obviously got used to that  in other sectors we've got better um  logistics which have kept people apart  from one another better ventilation of  uh of buildings and so on so  between industry and government we've  seen some forms of adaptation which have  meant that the economic costs  have not been as great but then  if we don't uh succeed in vaccinating  very large numbers of the population  globally and not just in certain  countries and these things  won't be fully effective it's a very  difficult choices for governments  because the economic costs are very  considerable you have obviously the  direct health costs of people getting  the disease um but you have huge  economic costs from making it more  difficult for people to work and to  consume and to produce and those have  real health costs themselves people  become ill  people are poorer then they can't access  healthcare themselves and so on so this  is a very difficult balancing act and  there aren't simple solutions  so what you're saying is that economic  inequality will worsen then michael  jacobs if if the situation continues as  is  economic inequality will worsen under  either leaving the the pandemic to go  its own way or  restricting the economy  much more deeply neither choice is good  for poor people because poor people have  fewer options always and so yes  inequality is likely to rise this is why  it's so important that governments um uh  extend the vaccination programs  particularly in the donation of vaccines  from rich countries to poorer countries  because in the end that is the only way  we will get a control of this and the  only way we can find some degree of  equitable uh outcome uh and i just want  to say about travel bans uh um  any form of travel restriction is an  economic cost to the countries involved  in that uh travel so even if you don't  ban people if you quarantine them if you  force them to have tests which they have  to pay for you will have a restriction  on the amount of travel that is unafraid  inevitable in a pandemic that travels  so quickly across borders so whether or  not it's actual bands or whether it's  just better  quarantining vaccination testing  processes that will impact on travel  with an economic cost  to what extent do you think this crisis  uh this pandemic has really been a  revelation on on weaknesses that exist  and how we all uh live together  absolutely and in fact  the weaknesses have been across board um  all types of communities and countries  all types of uh  strata and as far as uh social uh strata  is concerned  all of us have been exposed  in different ways now two things i like  to add to that  one is  um we do have tools at our disposal that  we need to use to slow this down so that  we can be able to perform um  adequate economic activities  one is testing we have rapid tests now  which give you results in a matter of  minutes and we need to use these more  effectively so that we can be able to  identify hot spots quickly and  put out  the spread as fast as possible before it  goes into other parts of the county  other parts of the world so effective  testing needs to be ramped up second is  vaccination  we need to vaccinate that way we can  reduce the number of people who are  going to be seriously ill and therefore  put less pressure on our health systems  and also have fewer people who can be  able to  spread the virus  more efficiently and finally is  information human beings by and large if  they're given correct information they  tend to react in a positive way they'll  always be that part of society that will  not be able to react in a positive way  the way that we transmit that  information how fast we do it and how  effectively it reaches the population is  critical in ensuring that the population  is  with  those who are responsible for guiding  and coordinating  right for this response and uh currently  we are not doing enough of providing  that information and we need to do it  more effectively okay on that note we'll  leave it there thank you so much for  joining us dr deepti gurdasani dr ahmad  dogwell oma and michael jacobs we  appreciate your time  thanks for watching you can see the  program again anytime by visiting our  website aljazeera.com for further  discu